#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702979} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702970}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & =\\lim_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13330 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097872 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097873 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 418 :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701640} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703945}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050107}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703836} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703827}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101973 :tutorial/content "Given the parametric equations of a curve, we can find its Cartesian equation by eliminating the parameter.\n\nFor instance, consider the parametric curve given by\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2 \\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nTo find the Cartesian equation of the curve, we start with the $x$ equation and solve for $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute this into our expression for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3.$\n\nHowever, our parametric curve was subject to the constraint $-2\\leq t \\leq 2.$ Since $x=2t,$ the corresponding constraint for $x$ is $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3, \\quad -4 \\leq x \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nThe graph of this curve is shown below:\n\n![](images/46/4697114d3ce40272a49dff794c4375301ae346e81b823049f587f8780172c413.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f03b65a-1f29-5eb4-839d-45eb293997a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3354 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101974 :tutorial/content "When parametric equations contain trigonometric equations, we can often eliminate the parameter using a trigonometric identity.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t\\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nFor the sine and cosine functions, we have the following Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+(3y)^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $4x^2 + 9y^2 = 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ac28915-be9b-5357-8577-3e4d1ab9a25d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3360 :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Curves Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720031} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720029}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720198} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720210}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720226} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720223}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107074 :tutorial/content "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." :tutorial/id #uuid "28eff4c1-2c88-5799-b15d-0ab9f27be09b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107075 :tutorial/content "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." :tutorial/id #uuid "67915540-ba40-5a4a-a7b0-6d81497d64c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2953 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107076 :tutorial/content "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n1\\. If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n  $$\n{\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}2} {a}_{1} & {\\color{red}2} {a}_{2} & {\\color{red}2} {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{array} \\right|} = {\\color{red}2} {\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{array} \\right|}\n$$\n\n2\\. If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n  $$\n{\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}{a}_{1}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{2}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{b}_{3}} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{array} \\right|} = -  {\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{b}_{3}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}_{1}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{2}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{3}} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{array} \\right|}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41cd1550-dd91-5029-a397-fa1417d1e160" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2954 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107077 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}-{a}_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}+{a}_{3}\\begin{vmatrix}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99f752d5-13c8-5b3e-aeee-383770b0db33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2955 :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712685} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712682}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712834} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712832}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712742} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712797}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120999 :tutorial/content "For a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nthe arc length of the curve between $t=a$ and $t=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, if we express our curve as a vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t)\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nwe can express the arc length of the curve in a more compact form as follows:\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that this is equivalent to the original arc length formula. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}, \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculating the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| =  \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2},\n$$\n\nand thus the integral of $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ with respect to $t$ over $[a,b]$ gives the arc length of the curve.\n\nFinally, note that we can use this arc length formula to find a curve's length in two and three dimensions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e4b94ce2-2b54-5700-81a5-6029c237a38e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2373 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121000 :tutorial/content "Given a curve $C$ parametrized by a function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, we can approximate the arc length by breaking up the curve into many small line segments and adding up their lengths.\n\nTo do this, we first choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\cdots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\nThen, we can approximate the arc length as the sum of the lengths of the line segments:\n\n$$\nL \\approx \\, \\big\\| \\overline{P_0P_1} \\big\\| + \\big\\| \\overline{P_1P_2} \\big\\| + \\ldots + \\big\\| \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n} \\big\\|.\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & \\approx \\,‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{0}{P}_{1}}‖+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{1}{P}_{2}}‖+…+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{n-1}{P}_{n}}‖ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Riemann sum, and the closer the points $P_k$ are to each other, the better our approximation will be. So, in the limit as $n\\to\\infty$ (or $\\Delta t_k \\to 0),$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}  \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k=\\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "236bdfbf-c5c3-5dca-9b00-44ccbf1509b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2374 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Arc Length Formula"}]}
